1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,228
Every animation you have ever
watched in a math video is,

2
00:00:03,228 --> 00:00:05,503
underneath, a list of instructions.

3
00:00:05,503 --> 00:00:07,860
Put a dot here. Turn it into a circle.

4
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:10,786
Now fold that circle into a square.

5
00:00:10,786 --> 00:00:13,038
What if you could write
those instructions yourself,

6
00:00:13,038 --> 00:00:16,667
and watch them come alive
the moment you finish typing?

7
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:17,840
This is Manim,

8
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,263
the Python library behind a whole
genre of animated math explanations.

9
00:00:22,263 --> 00:00:24,469
And this is ManimOnline,

10
00:00:24,469 --> 00:00:28,811
which runs a Manim-compatible engine
entirely inside your web browser.

11
00:00:28,811 --> 00:00:31,238
No install. No server doing the work.

12
00:00:31,238 --> 00:00:33,293
You stop typing, and a moment later,

13
00:00:33,293 --> 00:00:34,967
it plays.

14
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:37,800
In this series we will
start from a single square,

15
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,017
and build up to curves, equations,

16
00:00:40,017 --> 00:00:42,897
graphs, cameras that fly
through three dimensions,

17
00:00:42,897 --> 00:00:46,380
and animations that respond
to their own changing numbers.

18
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:49,526
Then, for the curious,
we will open the hood:

19
00:00:49,526 --> 00:00:52,545
how Python can run in
a browser tab at all,

20
00:00:52,545 --> 00:00:56,388
how you rebuild an animation engine and
prove that it behaves the same as the

21
00:00:56,388 --> 00:00:59,998
original, and how a link,
or a QR code like this one,

22
00:00:59,998 --> 00:01:02,600
can carry an entire program.

23
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:05,967
Every few minutes, a code like
this will appear in the corner.

24
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:09,659
Scan it, and the exact scene on
screen opens in your browser,

25
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:11,609
ready for you to change.

26
00:01:11,609 --> 00:01:12,435
Let's begin.
